      c2: { title:"Data Splits, Features & Model Capacity", intro:"Underfitting and overfitting are the central challenge of ML. This chapter introduces train/test splits, the bias-variance trade-off, and the idea of model capacity for balancing both.", sections:[{heading:"Why Split Data?",body:"A model evaluated on the same data it trained on appears artificially good — it memorised the answer. Holding out a test set gives an honest estimate of performance on unseen data.",bullets:["Training set: what the model learns from","Test set: honest, unseen evaluation","Validation set: tuning hyperparameters","Never tune on the test set"]},{heading:"Bias vs Variance",body:"Bias is error from an oversimplified model that misses patterns (underfitting). Variance is error from a model too sensitive to training noise (overfitting). The trade-off balances both.",bullets:["High bias = underfitting, too simple","High variance = overfitting, too complex","Total error = bias + variance + noise","Goal: minimise total generalisation error"]},{heading:"Model Capacity",body:"Capacity is how complex a model can be. Low capacity underfits; excessive capacity overfits. Regularisation and more data help you raise capacity without overfitting.",bullets:["Low capacity: underfits complex data","High capacity: may memorise noise","More data reduces variance","Regularisation tames high capacity"]}], tip:"If training error is high but test error is similar, you are underfitting — add complexity." },
