      c2: { title:"Decision Trees & Ensemble Methods", intro:"Decision trees split data into ever-purer regions using feature thresholds. Single trees overfit, so ensembles — random forests and boosting — combine many trees for robustness.", sections:[{heading:"How a Tree Decides",body:"A tree asks a sequence of feature-based questions (e.g., 'is age > 40?'). Each split selects the feature that best reduces impurity, usually measured by entropy or Gini impurity.",bullets:["Root to leaf = sequence of questions","Splits reduce impurity","Entropy / Gini guide feature choice","Leaves hold the prediction"]},{heading:"Why Trees Overfit",body:"Given enough depth, a tree can memorise every training example. This produces high variance. Limiting depth, requiring minimum samples per leaf, and pruning combat overfitting.",bullets:["Deep trees memorise training noise","Limit max depth","Require a minimum leaf size","Prune branches that add little value"]},{heading:"Ensembles Improve Stability",body:"Random forests average many trees trained on random subsets and features, reducing variance. Gradient boosting builds trees sequentially, each correcting the previous errors, reducing bias.",bullets:["Random forest: average of many trees","Boosting: correct previous mistakes","Bagging cuts variance","Boosting cuts bias"]}], tip:"Start with a shallow tree, then compare against a random forest as your reference model." },
