      c1: { title:"Cross-Validation & Confusion Matrices", intro:"A single train/test split can be misleading. Cross-validation uses data repeatedly for robust estimates, while the confusion matrix gives a complete view of classification errors.", sections:[{heading:"K-Fold Cross-Validation",body:"Split data into K folds. Train on K-1 folds, validate on the held-out fold, rotate until every fold is used once for validation. Average the scores for a robust estimate.",bullets:["Data split into K folds","Each fold used once as validation","Train on remaining K-1 folds","Report mean and spread of scores"]},{heading:"The Confusion Matrix",body:"For binary classifiers, four outcomes: true positive (TP), true negative (TN), false positive (FP, type I), false negative (FN, type II). Layout guides derived metrics.",bullets:["TP: positive predicted positive","FP: negative predicted positive","FN: positive predicted negative","TN: negative predicted negative"]},{heading:"Accuracy vs the Matrix",body:"Accuracy = (TP+TN)/total. It can mislead on imbalanced data — predicting the majority class wins while failing on the minority. The confusion matrix exposes this.",bullets:["Accuracy: correct / total","Imbalanced data skews accuracy","Matrix reveals per-class errors","Pair accuracy with other metrics"]}], tip:"On imbalanced data, never judge a model by accuracy alone — inspect the confusion matrix." },
      c2: { title:"Precision, Recall, F1 & ROC", intro:"Precision and recall view classification through false positives and false negatives. F1 balances both; ROC-AUC summarises performance across thresholds.", sections:[{heading:"Precision & Recall",body:"Precision = TP/(TP+FP): of positive predictions, how many are correct. Recall = TP/(TP+FN): of actual positives, how many are found. They trade off directly.",bullets:["Precision focuses on false positives","Recall focuses on false negatives","Raising the decision threshold → higher precision","Lowering it → higher recall"]},{heading:"The F1 Score",body:"F1 is the harmonic mean of precision and recall, penalising extreme imbalance. It is preferred when both classes matter and data is uneven.",bullets:["F1 = 2*P*R / (P+R)","Harmonic mean: balances both","Better than accuracy on imbalance","Drops sharply if either is low"]},{heading:"ROC & AUC",body:"ROC plots true positive rate against false positive rate across thresholds. AUC summarises the whole curve: higher AUC = better ranking. AUC 0.5 = random.",bullets:["Each threshold = one point","TPR = recall, FPR = false alarm","AUC = area under curve","AUC near 1 is excellent"]}], tip:"For medical screening, favour high recall (don't miss cases); for spam filters, favour precision (don't block real mail)." },
